description:
# Chunked or native encodeBase64 for large paste / OSC 52

## Background
ISA ISC-18. ANALYSIS BUG-04.

`terminal-attachment.ts` builds a binary string with per-byte `+= String.fromCharCode(byte)` then `btoa`. That is O(n²) in worst-case rope behavior and fails around ~100KB in V8. Large paste and OSC 52 clipboard payloads hit this on the browser attachment path.

ISC-8/9 replay and input flush tests must remain the oracle. Do not change WS framing except the encoder.

## Technical approach
```
function encodeBase64(bytes: Uint8Array): string {
  if (typeof (bytes as { toBase64?: () => string }).toBase64 === "function") {
    return (bytes as { toBase64(): string }).toBase64();
  }
  const CHUNK = 8192;
  let binary = "";
  for (let i = 0; i < bytes.length; i += CHUNK) {
    binary += String.fromCharCode(...bytes.subarray(i, i + CHUNK));
  }
  return btoa(binary);
}
```
If a matching `decode` exists for tests, leave it unless it has the same bug.

Export `encodeBase64` if needed for direct unit tests (or test through the attachment helper already used).

## Success criteria
- 100KiB+ buffer encodes successfully
- Small buffers still match `btoa` of the same bytes
- Existing attachment tests pass (queued input, replay order)

## Test plan
- New unit: 100KiB filled Uint8Array; decode back or length check of base64
- Existing `terminal-attachment.test.ts` all green
- `npm test`

## Considerations
Spread of 8192 args is safe. Do not use `String.fromCharCode.apply` on the whole buffer. Coordinate with replay-insert bead — both edit `terminal-attachment.ts`; implement encode first or same agent.
